# @extern emits extern fn zebra_probe_shape
# @extern emits-not unreachable; // abstract
#
# TIGHTENED, not relaxed. This read `emits-not unreachable` and matched a bare
# `unreachable` in the stdlib PREAMBLE -- the bootstrap emits the whole runtime
# into one file, so an unqualified word is not a statement about the user's
# function. `unreachable; // abstract` is the exact string the abstract arm
# writes, which is what the probe always meant: this declaration must not come
# out as an abstract stub. Verified the file contains ZERO of them and exactly
# one bare `unreachable`, so the old form was a false positive rather than a
# finding being suppressed.
# The mechanism, not the behaviour. `extern fn NAME(...)` must appear in the
# emitted Zig and `unreachable` must NOT -- the latter is today's wrong output
# and would still compile, so only reading the emit can tell the two apart.
extern def zebra_probe_shape(a: int32): int32

def main()
    print("declared")
